    "question": {
      ":question/id": "67a27f6b-6d71-5e1a-b18e-42f8f07e6f84",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(3)=-1\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{2}=-1-(-3)=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{3}=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(2)+(3)=1\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{1}=1-(-2)-(3)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=0$, ${x}_{2}=2$, and ${x}_{3}=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $\u27e80,2,3\u27e9.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
